# Local machine config for the linux/ scripts. Copy this to fgo.env (or run
# ./setup.sh, which does it for you) and fill in your own values - fgo.env is
# specific to your machine and shouldn't be shared/committed as-is; this
# .example file is the one meant to travel with the project.
#
# Plain shell assignments, sourced directly by lib/common.sh - anything set
# here overrides an already-exported environment variable of the same name.

# Linux path to the folder holding App/ and Server/ - e.g. the drive_c path
# inside your Wine prefix, or wherever you unzipped/mapped the install.
FGO_INSTALL_ROOT="/path/to/your/FGO Arcade folder"

# The Wine prefix this install runs in (only needed for fgo-launcher.sh, and
# for start-fgo-local-server.sh if you use the bundled Windows MariaDB).
WINEPREFIX="/path/to/your/wine/prefix"

# wine binary to use. Defaults to "wine" (whatever resolves on PATH) if unset.
# Must be a plain wine-compatible binary, or a Proton build's own wine (e.g.
# .../compatibilitytools.d/<build>/files/bin/wine) - fgo-launcher.sh calls it
# as `"$FGO_WINE" "$exe_path" args...`. A launcher-wrapper CLI (one that
# expects e.g. `mytool run <exe> --prefix ... --proton ...` instead of a
# direct `mytool <exe> args...` call) will misparse the first argument.
# Plain system wine is known not to work for this game at all (fails before
# the window is even created); use a Proton build.
#FGO_WINE="wine"

# python3 interpreter for the scripts and the ARTEMiS server itself.
# setup.sh points this at ./venv/bin/python automatically - only set this by
# hand if you want a different interpreter/venv.
#FGO_PYTHON="/path/to/venv/bin/python"

# Folder holding manifest.json and payload/ for apply-en-patch.sh (the
# English text patch package). Defaults to this project's own parent folder
# (the original distribution shipped linux/ alongside manifest.json/payload/
# as siblings) - only set this if you keep the patch package somewhere else,
# e.g. a separate downloaded release folder. Lets `apply-en-patch.sh` run
# with no flags at all, the same way FGO_INSTALL_ROOT does for --install-root.
#FGO_PACKAGE_ROOT="/path/to/your/FGOAC-scooby-vX.Y.Z folder"

# Ports fix-server-config.sh applies to a fresh/regenerated install (see
# that script). Defaults already match this project's own established
# values (777 is a privileged port Linux won't let an unprivileged process
# bind, so http moved to 8777 - see the root README's Configuration
# section) - only set these if your setup genuinely needs different ones.
#FGO_SERVER_HOST="auto"
#FGO_SERVER_HTTP_PORT="8777"
#FGO_SERVER_BILLING_PORT="9999"
#FGO_SERVER_AIME_PORT="7777"
#FGO_SERVER_DB_PORT="3306"

# Database connection fix-server-config.sh applies to core.yaml. Unlike the
# ports above, the game's own config tool never manages these - a fresh/
# regenerated install silently reverts to the factory default (the bundled
# local MariaDB at 127.0.0.1) with no way to reapply a remote MariaDB setup
# short of hand-editing the YAML every time. Leave unset to keep whatever
# core.yaml already has (including the factory default) - only set the ones
# you actually want to override; each is independent.
#FGO_DB_HOST="192.168.1.100"
#FGO_DB_USER="your-mariadb-username"
#FGO_DB_PASSWORD="your-mariadb-password"
#FGO_DB_NAME="your-database-name"

# Where fgo-launcher.sh keeps the compiled shader cache (App/shader-cache-rN/
# in the install, normally lost on every fresh/regenerated install - a cold
# recompile of ~1700 shaders takes 45-90s, a warm one ~5-8s). Defaults to
# ~/.cache/fgoac-linux/shader-cache if unset. fgo-launcher.sh moves the
# game's own cache dir here the first time it sees a real (non-symlink) one,
# symlinks it back, and relinks it proactively on every future run/regen -
# no manual step needed either way.
#FGO_SHADER_CACHE_DIR="$HOME/.cache/fgoac-linux/shader-cache"

# Run the whole game session inside gamescope (a nested compositor) instead
# of directly on your desktop's window manager. Works both windowed and
# fullscreen. Left disabled by default.
#FGO_GAMESCOPE="0"
#FGO_GAMESCOPE_FULLSCREEN="0"

# gamescope --backend to use. Unset = "sdl" when windowed, gamescope's own
# auto-detect when fullscreen. Windowed on the auto-picked Wayland backend
# freezes the game after its first frame and never shows a window (seen on
# KDE Plasma Wayland); SDL renders normally. With sdl, the launcher also puts
# SDL on X11 (XWayland) - its native-Wayland window can lose its connection
# at startup, leaving no window. Only set this to override.
#FGO_GAMESCOPE_BACKEND=""

# AMD FSR upscaling under gamescope (gamescope's own compositor-side FSR
# 1.0 - vendor-independent, works on any GPU). The game still renders at
# FGO_RESOLUTION_WIDTH/HEIGHT above; gamescope then upscales that to
# FGO_GAMESCOPE_OUTPUT_WIDTH/HEIGHT. Leaving the output size unset (or equal
# to the render size) means no upscaling happens even with FSR turned on -
# there has to be a real gap between the two for FSR to do anything.
#FGO_GAMESCOPE_FSR="0"
#FGO_GAMESCOPE_SHARPNESS="5"
#FGO_GAMESCOPE_OUTPUT_WIDTH=""
#FGO_GAMESCOPE_OUTPUT_HEIGHT=""

# Raw passthrough for any extra gamescope flags not otherwise exposed above
# (e.g. "--backend sdl" or "--force-windows-fullscreen"), space-separated,
# appended as-is to the gamescope invocation. Mainly useful for one-off
# troubleshooting.
#FGO_GAMESCOPE_EXTRA_ARGS=""

# Experimental: allow Target FPS above 60 (90/120/144). fgohook.dll hard-clamps
# anything above 60 ("experimental engine hooks suspended after UI/input
# regression"); with this set, the launcher writes a one-byte-patched copy,
# App/fgohook-highfps.dll, and injects it instead. The original fgohook.dll is
# never modified, and only the exact build the patch was made for is patched
# (linux/tools/patch_fgohook_highfps.py). Menus/touch may misbehave - that's why
# the hook's author switched it off. Leave unset for the normal 60 FPS.
#FGO_UNLOCK_HIGH_FPS="0"

# GUI only ("Live log in a terminal" checkbox next to Save & Play): on Play, the
# launcher's output goes to <install>/logs/fgo-console.log and a terminal opens
# running `tail -F` on it - useful when the GUI was started by double-click with
# no terminal attached. Closing that terminal doesn't stop the game. Terminal
# choice: xdg-terminal-exec, $TERMINAL, KDE's default terminal, then konsole/
# wezterm/kitty/alacritty/gnome-terminal/foot/xfce4-terminal/xterm.
#FGO_GUI_LOG_TERMINAL="0"

# ALL.Net accounting report (GUI: Server tab). amdaemon sends a daily report
# at accounting-report.start-time (prefix alib.conf, default 0700); booting
# shortly before it can hang the startup screen at "ALL.Net : WAIT (A, BUSY)"
# until you restart after that time. FGO_BILLING_TLS10=1 lets the report
# through the billing port (TLS 1.0 - ARTEMiS's index.py is patched on server
# start, and reverted when 0). FGO_ACCOUNTING_REPORT_TIME (HHMM) is written
# into alib.conf on each launch - pick an hour you never play, e.g. 0200.
#FGO_BILLING_TLS10="0"
#FGO_ACCOUNTING_REPORT_TIME="0700"

# A local checkout of the scooby launcher's own source, if you have one -
# lets the Deck tab show English card names and Craft Essence effect text,
# read from its src/FGOLocalPlatform.CardNames.json/CraftEffects.json. Not
# required: without it (or without a checkout at all), the Deck tab still
# works, just with untranslated (Japanese) names and no effect text.
#FGO_SCOOBY_SRC="/path/to/your/scooby/checkout"
